This software contains cryptographic protocol constants (FRAME_MAGIC,
FRAME_VERSION, CHANNEL_INVITE_PREFIX, and associated KDF domain labels)
derived from a two-party commitment ceremony. These constants are
irreproducible and form part of the GhostWire network identity. Nodes
compiled without these constants cannot participate in a GhostWire mesh
network.

Genesis Ceremony (2026-03-17):
-------------------------------
The GhostWire genesis constants were produced through a two-party
commit-reveal ceremony between:
- Party A (Mike): Contributed entropy R_mike (random 32 bytes)
- Party B (Claude): Contributed entropy R_claude (random 32 bytes)

The genesis key was derived as:
  BLAKE3("ghostwire genesis ceremony v1" || R_mike || R_claude)

This derivation produced 16 domain-specific constants for network
identity, protocol framing, channel management, and key derivation.

CRITICAL LEGAL PROPERTY: One party's entropy (R_claude) was permanently
destroyed after the ceremony as part of the protocol design. This means:

1. No single party holds the full derivation path
2. The ceremony cannot be reproduced by any party
3. No master key exists that can be seized or compelled
4. The constants can only be transmitted, not regenerated

This architectural design is intentional and provides legal protection
against compelled disclosure. The developers cannot provide decryption
keys or reproduce the genesis constants because they do not exist in
any retrievable form.

Export Compliance:
------------------
GhostWire implements NIST FIPS 203 standardized encryption (ML-KEM-768).
Open-source encryption source code publicly available is exempt from
EAR restrictions under 15 CFR §740.13(e) (Encryption Note).

Built on: libp2p, vodozemac, ml-kem, axum, and other open source
components. See Cargo.toml for the full dependency tree and their
respective licenses.
